//
// Expected: f0f1 — the same fresh nonce, sent twice by the same principal in
// the same message, is fresh to its recipient over a guarded wire and not
// fresh over an open one.
//
// This looks like a quirk and is actually the correct answer. Freshness asks
// whether the value a principal is USING is guaranteed to differ between runs.
// Over a guarded wire Bob is holding what Alice computed, and Alice generated
// it. Over an open wire the attacker may substitute anything at all, including
// a value replayed verbatim out of an earlier run, so what Bob holds carries
// no guarantee whatsoever — his state resolves it to the bare constant, which
// transitively contains nothing, and the query fails.
//
// Every other freshness model in the tree keeps its subject inside one
// principal or behind a guard, so this effect is invisible in all of them and
// surprising the first time it is met. Pinning it here is what stops it from
// being "fixed" into f0f0, which would report a replayable value as fresh —
// the exact failure mode a freshness query exists to catch.

attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	generates nonce
	sealed = HASH(nonce)
	open_copy = HASH(nonce)
]

Alice -> Bob: [sealed], open_copy

principal Bob[
	_ = HASH(sealed)
	_ = HASH(open_copy)
]

queries[
	freshness? sealed
	freshness? open_copy
]
